  "track": "track-03",
  "title": "transactions isolation levels blocking locks deadlocks and row versioning",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "A transaction defines an atomic unit of work, but atomicity does not by itself prevent concurrent anomalies. Isolation level selects which interleavings readers can observe and what conflicts writers detect.",
    "Locking coordinates conflicting access; blocking is waiting, while deadlock is a cycle in which the engine chooses a victim. A transaction that holds locks too long increases contention and failure probability.",
    "Row-versioning isolation can reduce reader-writer blocking but consumes version-store space and does not eliminate writer conflicts or all consistency anomalies.",
    "RCSI and Snapshot isolation are different: RCSI generally gives each statement a committed snapshot, while Snapshot gives a transaction-level view and can reject conflicting updates.",
    "SQL Server 2025 optimized locking changes lock duration and volume under supported configurations; traditional hints and troubleshooting assumptions must be checked against actual deployment settings."
  ],
